+Let~$G$ be the submultiset of chords actually traversed by the strategy.
+Every active outside chord is used when its outside vertex is probed, whereas
+an auxiliary-chord copy is never used more than once.  Thus the multiset
+difference $H:=F\setminus G$ consists only of unused auxiliary chords.
+Let~$c_e$ be the number of traversals of backbone edge~$e$, including the
+final return to~$r$.
+
+First suppose that some backbone edge is unused.  If every backbone edge is
+unused, there is nothing to prove.  Otherwise, fix an edge~$e$ with $c_e>0$
+and go backwards around the backbone to the first unused edge~$e'$.
+Let~$I$ be the cyclic interval whose entering boundary edge is~$e'$ and whose
+leaving boundary edge is~$e$.  Every vertex in~$I$ has its outgoing backbone
+edge traversed.  The strategy takes such an edge only after exhausting the tail
+list and all auxiliary chords at its tail.  Hence no chord in~$H$ has its
+tail in~$I$, and therefore
+\[
+    \sum_{h\in H}\chi_I(h)\le0.
+\]
+Flow balance of the completed closed walk gives
+\[
+    c_e
+    =-\sum_{g\in G}\chi_I(g)
+    =-\sum_{f\in F}\chi_I(f)+\sum_{h\in H}\chi_I(h)
+    \le
+      \left|\sum_{f\in F}\chi_I(f)\right|
+    \le\Lambda(F)-1.
+\]
+The same bound is trivial for an unused edge.
+
+Now suppose that every backbone edge is used, and let~$e^*$ be the edge whose
+first use occurs last.  Before a backbone edge is first used, its tail list
+and all auxiliary chords at its tail have been exhausted.  Thus, by the first
+use of~$e^*$, every tail list and auxiliary stack has been exhausted, so
+$G=F$.  At this point every backbone vertex has been reached, and every
+non-root backbone vertex has been probed.
+The strategy then follows the backbone to~$r$ without using~$e^*$ again, and
+hence $c_{e^*}=1$.  Flow balance over the cyclic interval between
+any edge~$e$ and~$e^*$ gives
+\[
+    |c_e-c_{e^*}|
+    \le\max_I\left|\sum_{f\in F}\chi_I(f)\right|
+    =\Lambda(F)-1.
+\]
+Therefore $c_e\le\Lambda(F)$ for every backbone edge~$e$.
